Subject: Ask for a timing policy before retiming an existing project

Freecut MIT adaptation (FL-94). Matching a project to its first video,
or any other frame-rate change, used to rewrite the rate while every
clip, keyframe and marker kept its frame number, so an existing edit
silently played faster or slower. When the main timeline has content,
the person now chooses:

- keep-time: timeline frames move to the new rate exactly (integer
  ratio, nearest frame, clip ends rounded with their starts). A clip
  that rounding would overlap is pushed later by the overflow, with the
  butted clips after it; a rounded-up clip never reads past its source
  window; the last keyframe of a lane keeps the settled value. Media
  that read their source at the timeline rate are pinned to the old
  rate so they show the same pictures.
- keep-frames: the previous behaviour, now an explicit choice.

commitProjectMetadataChange refuses a rate change on content without a
policy, so every caller is covered. The new rate and the retimed
timeline are stored in one project write, and undo or redo of a rate
change stores the restored timeline with its rate, so a reload never
pairs a rate with frames written for another. The dialog re-checks for
content when it is submitted. A timeline drop reads its frame before
the prompt: the frame now keeps its second under keep-time, and a drop
whose timeline the prompt switched away from is abandoned rather than
placed on another timeline's track.

Colour needs no prompt: the working range follows the media (FL-97
owner decision, patch 0033).

